Problem 1.
Write a class to represent a two dimensional point (i.e. an
(x, y) coordinate). Write a subclass to represent a three dimensional point.
Write a subclass to represent a three dimensional colored point.

Problem 2.
Write a class Color to represent a color. A color is an intensity
from 0 to 255 of each of the three components, red, green and blue, and
Color has a method that returns each of them. Write subclasses for red,
green, blue, black, white, gray and cyan. Each subclass has a constructor
with no arguments that passes its own three intensities up with super. Add a
method Color.describe that returns the three values as text, and check that
every subclass has it without writing it again. Then answer two questions in
writing. Should Color be abstract? And what does a subclass of it inherit
that you did not have to write?

Problem 3.
Write an abstract class Shape with three abstract methods,
Shape.area, Shape.circumference and Shape.edges. Write a subclass for
each of these shapes: square, rectangle, triangle, circle and line. Then do
three things. Try to write new Shape() and copy the error the compiler gives
you. Put five shapes of different kinds in a Shape[] and print the total area
with one loop, which never asks what kind of shape it is holding. And decide
what Line.area returns, since a line has no area, and write down why you
chose that.

Problem 4.
Write a class to represent a person. A person has a name
and an age. Write a subclass to represent an employee. An employee has a
