job title and a salary. Write a subclass to represent a manager. A manager
has bonus which is added on top of his or her base salary. Add appropriate
constructors for each class. Write a method Employee.pay that returns what
the employee is paid, and override it in the manager so that it adds the bonus,
with @Override above it.

Problem 5.
Write a class to represent a parking meter. The parking meter
should have a method to insert coins and pay for x minutes. The parking rate
depends on whether it is weekday or weekend. Write an abstract class Rate
with one abstract method, Rate.costFor, which takes a number of minutes
and returns what it costs. Write two subclasses of Rate, one for weekdays
and one for weekends. The parking meter holds a Rate and asks it, so the
meter has no idea which of the two it is holding. Then write down what you
would have to change to add a holiday rate.

Problem 6.
Write an abstract class Entry to represent an entry in a file
system. Every entry has a name, a creation date and a size, and Entry.size
is abstract. Write two subclasses, one for a regular file and one for a directory.
A regular file knows its own size. A directory holds other entries, regular files
or directories, and its size is the sum of theirs. Add a method that returns
the oldest regular file of a directory and one that returns the newest.

